*Experiment - Use sed command to swap the first and second words of each line in a file*

*Command - sed -i 's/^ $[^ ]_$ $._$$/\2 \1/' http://name.txt*

*Explanation:*
- Sed - stream editor used to modify text
- -i - modifies the file directly
- ^ - indicates the beginning of a line
- $[^ ]_$ - matches and stores the first word
- $._$ - matches and stores the remaining text
- \2 \1 - reverses their order, putting the second part first
- http://name.txt - input text file

*Procedure -*
1) Open the terminal
2) Now, create a file named http://name.txt using the cat command and enter the names
3) Press ctrl + D to save the file and return to terminal
4) Use the following sed command to interchange the first and last names: sed -i 's/^ $[^ ]_$ $._$$/\2 \1/' http://name.txt
5) Display the modified file using: cat http://name.txt
6) Observe that the first and last names have been interchanged

*Result -* The first and last names were successfully interchanged using the sed command
